    49	\begin{document}
    50	\maketitle
    51	
    52	\begin{abstract}
    53	Preprocessing screening is often the most expensive part of a near-infrared
    54	spectroscopy calibration workflow.  It works because smoothing, derivatives,
    55	detrending and related filters change the spectral directions seen by PLS or
    56	Ridge regression, but a full external search repeatedly refits nearly the same
    57	linear model.  This paper studies the case where that search can be collapsed
    58	into one calibration step.
    59	
    60	For strict linear preprocessing operators, the transformed PLS
    61	cross-covariance satisfies
    62	$(\X\A\transpose)\transpose\Y=\A\X\transpose\Y$, and Ridge regression
    63	depends on the operator-induced kernel
    64	$\X\A\transpose\A\X\transpose$.  These identities allow a finite operator
    65	bank to be screened inside the model while retaining original-wavelength
    66	coefficients.  Sample-adaptive or fitted corrections such as SNV, MSC, EMSC
    67	and ASLS remain fold-local branches, not absorbed into the algebra.
    68	
    69	The study uses the full available AOM benchmark cohort: 61 regression rows in
    70	the manifest, 53 AOM-PLS paired datasets for variant screening, 52 paired
    71	AOM-Ridge datasets against Ridge, 36 completed PLS-HPO datasets, 35 completed
    72	Ridge-HPO datasets, and 16 classification rows.  The selected PLS variant,
    73	\texttt{ASLS-AOM-compact-cv5}, was comparable to the completed full
    74	preprocessing-HPO baseline (median RMSEP ratio 1.002 on $N=32$, 15/32 wins)
    75	and improved over default PLS on its broad overlap (0.985 on $N=52$, 33/52
    76	wins).  The selected Ridge variant, \texttt{AOMRidge-Blender-headline-spxy3},
    77	improved over Ridge-HPO (0.956 on $N=34$, 27/34 wins) and over default Ridge
    78	(0.913 on $N=52$, 44/52 wins).  The selected classifier,
    79	\texttt{AOM-PLS-DA-global-simpls-covariance}, improved balanced accuracy by
    80	0.159 on $N=13$ datasets with 12/13 wins.  The runtime gap is the practical
    81	result: PLS-HPO took a median total time of 984.90 s per completed run,
    82	whereas the selected AOM-PLS branch took 1.94 s.  Linear operator-adaptive
    83	calibration therefore gives comparable accuracy to exhaustive preprocessing
    84	screening on the completed subset, with orders-of-magnitude less fitting
    85	time for PLS.
    86	\end{abstract}
    87	
    88	\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} near-infrared spectroscopy; chemometrics;
    89	preprocessing selection; partial least squares; ridge regression; operator
    90	kernels; calibration.
    91	
    92	\section{Introduction}
    93	
    94	Near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS) is used because it is rapid,
    95	non-destructive and compatible with routine analytical deployment
    96	\citep{burns2007handbook,pasquini2018near}.  The calibration step is still
    97	fragile.  A model that works on raw spectra may fail after a different split,
    98	instrument or sample family, while the same regressor with a derivative,
    99	smoother or baseline correction can become usable.  In practice, much of the
   100	model-building effort is therefore spent screening preprocessing choices.
   101	
   102	The usual response is external search.  A calibration workflow enumerates
   103	normalizations, smoothers, derivative orders, baseline corrections and model
   104	hyperparameters, then chooses the best validated pipeline.  That workflow is
   105	straightforward and often effective, but it is slow.  It also hides a
   106	scientific question inside a large engineering loop: which spectral linear
   107	geometry does the calibration model need?
   108	
   109	This paper focuses on the part of preprocessing that has a simple algebraic
   110	answer.  If a preprocessing step is a fixed linear operator on the wavelength
   111	axis, it can be represented by a matrix $\A$ and moved inside PLS or Ridge
   112	calibration.  The model can then screen a bank of such operators in one fit
   113	rather than refitting every transformed data matrix as an external pipeline.
   114	This does not claim that every preprocessing step is linear.  SNV, MSC, EMSC
   115	and ASLS are fitted or sample-adaptive transformations; they remain
   116	fold-local branches in the experimental protocol.
   117	
   118	The contribution is deliberately narrow.  We derive the PLS covariance
   119	identity and the Ridge operator-kernel identity, instantiate them in AOM-PLS
   120	and AOM-Ridge, and test the resulting models against default PLS/Ridge and
   121	full cartesian preprocessing-HPO baselines.  The question is not whether a
   122	new nonlinear learner can win a benchmark.  The question is whether the
   123	preprocessing search already used with linear chemometric models can be made
   124	faster, simpler and easier to audit.
   125	
   126	\begin{figure}[t]
   127	  \centering
   128	  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_concept.pdf}
   129	  \caption{External preprocessing screening repeatedly fits transformed
   130	  pipelines.  Operator-adaptive calibration moves strict linear alternatives
   131	  inside the calibration model; fitted or sample-adaptive corrections remain
   132	  fold-local branches.}
   133	  \label{fig:concept}
   134	\end{figure}
   135	
   136	\section{Related work}
   137	
   138	Spectral preprocessing is a central part of chemometrics.  Common tools
   139	include Savitzky--Golay smoothing and derivatives
   140	\citep{savitzky1964smoothing}, Norris--Williams derivatives
   141	\citep{norris1976influence}, SNV \citep{barnes1989standard}, MSC
   142	\citep{geladi1985linearization}, EMSC \citep{martens1991extended} and
   143	penalized baseline smoothers \citep{eilers2003perfect,zhang2010baseline}.
   144	Reviews emphasize that these transformations remove different nuisance
   145	structures and should be chosen with the analytical setting in mind
   146	\citep{rinnan2009review}.
   147	
   148	PLS remains the reference linear method for many NIRS calibration problems
   149	\citep{geladi1986partial,wold2001pls}.  NIPALS and SIMPLS offer two useful
   150	views: one iterative and score-based, the other covariance-based
   151	\citep{dejong1993simpls}.  Ridge regression provides a complementary
   152	regularized linear model \citep{hoerl1970ridge}; in dual form it depends on
   153	the sample Gram matrix, which makes preprocessing a change of kernel geometry
   154	\citep{scholkopf2002learning,hastie2009elements}.
   155	
   156	This work combines these two lines.  It keeps the chemometric interpretability
   157	of linear calibration, but treats strict preprocessing alternatives as
   158	operators that can be selected inside the model rather than as independent
   159	outer-loop pipelines.
   160	
   161	\section{Linear-operator scope}
   162	
   163	Let $\X\in\R^{n\times p}$ be a centered spectral matrix, with rows as samples
   164	and columns as wavelengths.  A strict linear preprocessing operator is a fixed
   165	matrix $\A\in\R^{p\times p}$ applied to each row spectrum:
   166	\begin{equation}
   167	  \X_{\A}=\X\A\transpose .
   168	\end{equation}
   169	The operator is fixed once the wavelength grid and operator parameters are
   170	chosen.  It does not depend on the response, the validation fold, the current
   171	sample, or a reference spectrum estimated from the calibration set.
   172	
   173	The strict-linear bank used in the main experiments contains identity,
   174	Savitzky--Golay smoothers, Savitzky--Golay first and second derivatives,
   175	finite differences, and polynomial detrending projections.  The compact bank
   176	is intentionally small: it has nine operators, so internal CV selection
   177	evaluates a small number of candidate geometries rather than a large
   178	cartesian preprocessing grid.
   179	
   180	\begin{table}[t]
   181	  \centering
   182	  \caption{Operator-bank scope.  Strict linear operators are handled by the
   183	  AOM identities; fitted or sample-adaptive transformations remain explicit
   184	  fold-local branches.}
   185	  \label{tab:operators}
   186	  \small
   187	  \input{tables/table_operator_bank.tex}
   188	\end{table}
   189	
   190	The outside-scope examples are important.  SNV uses sample-specific centering
   191	and scaling.  MSC and EMSC estimate correction parameters relative to a
   192	reference.  ASLS estimates a baseline through an iterative asymmetric
   193	criterion.  These transformations are allowed in the benchmark, but only as
   194	branches fitted inside each calibration fold.  The selected PLS result uses
   195	such a branch followed by the compact strict-linear AOM bank.
   196	
   197	\section{Methods}
   198	
   199	\subsection{Covariance identity for one-shot AOM-PLS}
   200	
   201	For centered $\X$ and $\Y$, the transformed PLS cross-covariance for operator
   202	$\A_b$ is
   203	\begin{equation}
   204	  (\X\A_b\transpose)\transpose\Y
   205	  =
   206	  \A_b\X\transpose\Y .
   207	  \label{eq:cov_identity}
   208	\end{equation}
   209	Equation~\ref{eq:cov_identity} is the core simplification.  It means that a
   210	bank of strict linear preprocessings can be screened by acting on
   211	$\X\transpose\Y$, without fitting one full PLS model for every transformed
   212	matrix.
   213	
   214	The simulation check for this identity is direct: explicit transformed-space
   215	PLS and covariance-space AOM-PLS must select the same operator direction when
   216	the same centered fold and operator bank are supplied.  The check is used as
   217	an implementation guard, while the benchmark below evaluates the effect on
   218	held-out prediction.
   219	
   220	\subsection{AOM-PLS solvers and coefficient recovery}
   221	
   222	A standard PLS coefficient matrix can be written as
   223	\begin{equation}
   224	  \B=\mathbf W(\mathbf P\transpose\mathbf W)^+\mathbf Q\transpose ,
   225	\end{equation}
   226	where $\mathbf W$ are weights, $\mathbf P$ are spectral loadings,
   227	$\mathbf Q$ are response loadings and $+$ denotes the Moore--Penrose inverse.
   228	In AOM-PLS, a component may be selected in a transformed space.  If the
   229	transformed-space direction is $r_a$ under operator $\A_b$, its
   230	original-wavelength direction is
   231	\begin{equation}
   232	  z_a=\A_b\transpose r_a .
   233	\end{equation}
   234	With $\Z=[z_1,\ldots,z_k]$ and scores $\T=\X\Z$, the recovered coefficient
   235	matrix is
   236	\begin{equation}
   237	  \B=\Z(\mathbf P\transpose\Z)^+\mathbf Q\transpose .
   238	  \label{eq:aompls_coef}
   239	\end{equation}
   240	The fitted model is therefore still one linear calibration on the original
   241	wavelength grid.
   242	
   243	Two solver views are implemented.  The NIPALS-adjoint solver applies operator
   244	adjoints during iterative score extraction.  The SIMPLS-covariance solver
   245	uses Equation~\ref{eq:cov_identity} directly in covariance space.  The main
   246	regression result uses the compact CV5 branch with ASLS fitted fold-locally
   247	before strict-linear AOM selection.  The default bank, SIMPLS-only variants,
   248	and other non-selected AOM variants are reported in the supplementary
   249	material.
   250	
   251	The compact bank was chosen from the selector diagnostics, not by adding an
   252	unbounded operator catalogue.  In the refreshed diagnostics, the three most
   253	frequent strict operators
   254	(\texttt{sg\_smooth\_w21\_p3}, \texttt{sg\_d1\_w21\_p3} and
   255	\texttt{sg\_d1\_w11\_p2}) account for 54.7\% of compact-bank component
   256	selections, while identity remains selected on 8.7\% of components.  The
   257	operator bank is therefore diverse enough to adapt, but small enough to keep
   258	the selection problem stable.
   259	
