    46	\section{Notation, Scope and Complete Derivations}
    47	
    48	\subsection{Strict linear operators}
    49	
    50	Let $\X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ be a spectral matrix and
    51	$\Y\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times q}$ a response matrix.  A strict linear spectral
    52	operator $\A_b\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times p}$ acts on row spectra as
    53	\begin{equation}
    54	  \X_b=\X\A_b\transpose .
    55	\end{equation}
    56	The operator is fixed once the wavelength grid and hyperparameters are fixed.
    57	The compact bank contains identity, Savitzky--Golay smoothers and
    58	derivatives, finite differences and polynomial detrending projections.
    59	
    60	Sample-adaptive or fitted transformations do not satisfy this definition.
    61	SNV uses sample-specific statistics, MSC and EMSC estimate correction
    62	parameters relative to a reference, and ASLS solves an asymmetric baseline
    63	problem.  They are valid preprocessing branches, but they are fitted inside
    64	each calibration fold and are not inserted into the strict-linear operator
    65	identities.
    66	
    67	\subsection{AOM-PLS covariance identity}
    68	
    69	For centered $\X$ and $\Y$, standard PLS can be written with weights
    70	$\mathbf W$, loadings $\mathbf P$ and response loadings $\mathbf Q$:
    71	\begin{equation}
    72	  \B=\mathbf W(\mathbf P\transpose\mathbf W)^+\mathbf Q\transpose .
    73	\end{equation}
    74	For a fixed operator $\A_b$,
    75	\begin{equation}
    76	  (\X\A_b\transpose)\transpose\Y
    77	  =
    78	  \A_b\X\transpose\Y .
    79	  \label{eq:sup_cov_identity}
    80	\end{equation}
    81	Equation~\ref{eq:sup_cov_identity} is the algebraic reason why strict-linear
    82	preprocessing alternatives can be evaluated in covariance space.  The
    83	SIMPLS-covariance implementation acts on $\X\transpose\Y$ directly.  The
    84	NIPALS-adjoint implementation applies $\A_b$ and $\A_b\transpose$ during the
    85	iterative extraction without storing every transformed design matrix.
    86	
    87	If a transformed-space direction for component $a$ is $r_a$, the
    88	original-wavelength direction is
    89	\begin{equation}
    90	  z_a=\A_b\transpose r_a .
    91	\end{equation}
    92	With $\Z=[z_1,\ldots,z_k]$ and $\T=\X\Z$, the final coefficient matrix is
    93	\begin{equation}
    94	  \B=\Z(\mathbf P\transpose\Z)^+\mathbf Q\transpose .
    95	  \label{eq:sup_aompls_coef}
    96	\end{equation}
    97	Thus the fitted AOM-PLS object predicts from original spectra and can expose
    98	coefficients on the original wavelength grid.
    99	
   100	\subsection{AOM-Ridge kernels}
   101	
   102	For centered $\X_c$ and $\Y_c$, a single operator induces
   103	\begin{align}
   104	  \mathbf Z_b &= \X_c\A_b\transpose,\\
   105	  \K_b &= \mathbf Z_b\mathbf Z_b\transpose
   106	       = \X_c\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose,\\
   107	  \mathbf C_b &= (\K_b+\alpha\mathbf I_n)^{-1}\Y_c,
   108	\end{align}
   109	and the recovered original-space coefficient matrix is
   110	\begin{equation}
   111	  \bm\beta_b=\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose\mathbf C_b .
   112	\end{equation}
   113	For weighted operator mixtures,
   114	\begin{equation}
   115	  \K=\sum_{b=1}^{m}s_b^2\X_c\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose,
   116	  \qquad
   117	  \mathbf M=\sum_{b=1}^{m}s_b^2\A_b\transpose\A_b,
   118	\end{equation}
   119	so
   120	\begin{equation}
   121	  \bm\beta=\mathbf M\X_c\transpose(\K+\alpha\mathbf I_n)^{-1}\Y_c.
   122	\end{equation}
   123	This is the Ridge counterpart of AOM-PLS: PLS adapts supervised
   124	cross-covariance directions, while Ridge adapts the regularized sample
   125	geometry.
   126	
   127	\subsection{Ridge selectors not promoted to the main text}
   128	
   129	The main manuscript keeps only the Blender selector.  The full Ridge family
   130	also includes global compact selection, branch-specific compact variants,
   131	local-neighbourhood selectors and multi-branch kernel mixtures.  Global
   132	selectors choose one operator geometry for the whole calibration set.  Local
   133	selectors restrict the geometry to neighbourhoods such as $k=50$ nearest
   134	calibration samples.  Multi-branch kernels combine several branch geometries
   135	with shrinkage.  These variants are useful for understanding the design
   136	space, but their refreshed paired statistics do not beat Blender on the main
   137	Ridge denominators.
   138	
   139	\subsection{FastAOM derivation}
   140	
   141	FastAOM is treated here as an exploration of alternative AOM forms, not as a
   142	main result.  It defines a chain as an ordered composition of primitive
   143	strict operators.  The grammar assigns roles to operators:
   144	baseline/projection, smoothing, derivative and projection-mask operations.  A
   145	depth cap limits the number of composed operators, and grammar pruning removes
   146	invalid or redundant role sequences before screening.
   147	
   148	For each fold-local base $B_j(\X)$ and chain $s$, FastAOM computes
   149	\begin{equation}
   150	  \mathrm{score}(j,s)
   151	  =
   152	  \frac{\left\|A_s B_j(\X_c)\transpose y_c\right\|_2^2}
   153	       {\left\|B_j(\X_c)A_s\transpose\right\|_F^2
   154	        \left\|y_c\right\|_2^2}.
   155	\end{equation}
   156	With a truncated SVD $B_j(\X_c)\approx U_j\Sigma_jV_j\transpose$ and
   157	$F_{j,s}=A_sV_j\Sigma_j$, the screened kernel is approximated by
   158	\begin{equation}
   159	  \K_{j,s}
   160	  =
   161	  B_j(\X_c)A_s\transpose A_s B_j(\X_c)\transpose
   162	  \approx U_j C_{j,s}U_j\transpose,
   163	  \qquad
   164	  C_{j,s}=F_{j,s}\transpose F_{j,s}.
   165	\end{equation}
   166	
