   260	\subsection{AOM-Ridge operator kernels}
   261	
   262	For centered spectra $\X_c$ and response $\Y_c$, Ridge regression can be
   263	solved in the dual.  Operator $\A_b$ induces the kernel
   264	\begin{equation}
   265	  \K_b=\X_c\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose .
   266	  \label{eq:ridge_kernel}
   267	\end{equation}
   268	For regularization $\alpha>0$,
   269	\begin{equation}
   270	  \mathbf C=(\K_b+\alpha\mathbf I_n)^{-1}\Y_c,
   271	  \qquad
   272	  \bm\beta_b=\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose\mathbf C .
   273	  \label{eq:ridge_beta}
   274	\end{equation}
   275	Weighted operator mixtures are obtained by replacing $\K_b$ with
   276	\begin{equation}
   277	  \K=\sum_b s_b^2\X_c\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose,
   278	\end{equation}
   279	which gives an original-space coefficient matrix through the matching
   280	operator metric
   281	$\sum_b s_b^2\A_b\transpose\A_b$.
   282	
   283	The main Ridge result uses the Blender selector
   284	(\texttt{AOMRidge-Blender-headline-spxy3}).  It combines several strong
   285	operator geometries through internal validation rather than committing to one
   286	noisy winner.  GCV, local-neighbourhood and global compact selectors are
   287	mathematically described in the supplement; only Blender is kept in the main
   288	results because it has the best refreshed win-rate and median ratio on the
   289	largest paired denominators.
   290	
   291	\begin{figure}[t]
   292	  \centering
   293	  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_math.pdf}
   294	  \caption{AOM mathematical structure.  AOM-PLS screens operators through
   295	  cross-covariances; AOM-Ridge screens operator-induced kernels.  Both recover
   296	  coefficients on the original wavelength grid.}
   297	  \label{fig:math}
   298	\end{figure}
