   342	\section{Results}
   343	
   344	\subsection{PLS regression}
   345	
   346	The selected AOM-PLS regression variant is
   347	\texttt{ASLS-AOM-compact-cv5}.  It was selected from the largest AOM-PLS
   348	screening denominator and then compared in the main text only with the
   349	pre-registered linear references.  Against PLS-default, it reaches a median
   350	RMSEP ratio of 0.985 on $N=52$ datasets with 33/52 wins.  Against the
   351	completed PLS-HPO subset, it is essentially tied: median ratio 1.002 on
   352	$N=32$, 15/32 wins.
   353	
   354	This is the intended interpretation.  Full external preprocessing-HPO can
   355	recover the benefit of careful preprocessing, but the gain is obtained by
   356	repeatedly fitting the linear calibration under a much larger search budget.
   357	AOM-PLS makes the strict-linear part of the same decision inside one
   358	coefficient-bearing calibration object.
   359	
   360	\subsection{Ridge regression}
   361	
   362	The selected Ridge variant is
   363	\texttt{AOMRidge-Blender-headline-spxy3}.  It improves strongly over default
   364	Ridge: median RMSEP ratio 0.913 on $N=52$ datasets with 44/52 wins and
   365	Holm-adjusted $p=2.8\times10^{-6}$.  It also improves over the completed
   366	Ridge-HPO subset, with median ratio 0.956 on $N=34$, 27/34 wins and
   367	$p=0.011$.
   368	
   369	The Ridge result supports the operator-kernel view.  In Ridge, preprocessing
   370	changes the regularized sample geometry.  Blender is the best main selector
   371	because it combines strong operator geometries through internal validation
   372	while preserving a single deployable Ridge model.
