   457	\section{Discussion}
   458	
   459	The benchmark supports a simple engineering conclusion: when preprocessing
   460	alternatives are strict linear operators, screening them outside the model is
   461	often unnecessary.  AOM-PLS folds the operator choice into the PLS
   462	cross-covariance calculation, and AOM-Ridge folds it into the Ridge kernel.
   463	The resulting calibration remains linear and auditable.
   464	
   465	The strongest use case is the one common in NIRS: small to moderate
   466	calibration sets, noisy validation folds and many plausible preprocessing
   467	choices.  In that regime, an exhaustive external search spends most of its
   468	time refitting variations of the same linear model.  Compact AOM-PLS reduces
   469	that cost by orders of magnitude while remaining close to the completed
   470	PLS-HPO baseline, and AOM-Ridge improves over both default Ridge and
   471	Ridge-HPO on the paired completed subset.
   472	
   473	The scope is also clear.  AOM algebra does not absorb sample-adaptive
   474	transforms.  SNV, MSC, EMSC and ASLS must be fitted inside folds.  This is why
   475	the selected PLS result is described as an ASLS branch followed by strict
   476	linear AOM, not as ASLS being part of the operator identity.  When such
   477	branches dominate a dataset, they should remain explicit and audited.
   478	
   479	The HPO comparisons should be read with their denominators.  Cartesian-HPO
   480	completed on 36 PLS and 35 Ridge datasets, not on the full regression
   481	manifest.  AOM comparisons cover broader paired denominators, but the
   482	head-to-head statements against HPO are only made on the completed overlaps.
   483	This avoids extrapolating from unfinished compute campaigns.
   484	
   485	\section{Reproducibility}
   486	
   487	The numerical artifacts are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:software}.  The
   488	tables and matplotlib figures in this manuscript are regenerated from the
   489	review result workspaces by the aggregation and figure scripts in
   490	\texttt{paper\_aom/review}.  The live cohort denominators are those described
   491	in Section~\ref{sec:datasets}.
   492	
   493	\begin{table}[t]
   494	  \centering
   495	  \caption{Software artifacts and validation status.}
   496	  \label{tab:software}
   497	  \small
   498	  \input{tables/table_software.tex}
   499	\end{table}
   500	
   501	\section{Conclusion}
   502	
   503	Preprocessing screening for linear NIRS calibration works, but it is often
   504	implemented as an expensive outer loop.  For strict linear operators, PLS and
   505	Ridge provide identities that move the operator choice inside the calibration
   506	itself.  On the available AOM benchmark, the selected AOM-PLS and AOM-Ridge
   507	variants are comparable to or better than full preprocessing-HPO on completed
   508	overlaps, with a large time advantage for PLS.  The useful scientific message
   509	is therefore modest and practical: keep fitted corrections fold-local, fold
   510	strict linear operators into the model, and report every comparison on its
   511	paired denominator.
   512	
   513	\section*{Data and code availability}
   514	
   515	The public software entry point is \texttt{nirs4all} at
