    96	\section{Introduction}
    97	
    98	Near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS) is used because it is rapid,
    99	non-destructive and compatible with routine analytical deployment
   100	\citep{burns2007handbook,pasquini2018near}.  The calibration step is still
   101	fragile.  A model that works on raw spectra may fail after a different split,
   102	instrument or sample family, while the same regressor with a derivative,
   103	smoother or baseline correction can become usable.  In practice, much of the
   104	model-building effort is therefore spent screening preprocessing choices.
   105	
   106	The usual response is external search.  A calibration workflow enumerates
   107	normalizations, smoothers, derivative orders, baseline corrections and model
   108	hyperparameters, then chooses the best validated pipeline.  That workflow is
   109	straightforward and often effective, but it is slow.  It also hides a
   110	scientific question inside a large engineering loop: which spectral linear
   111	geometry does the calibration model need?
   112	
   113	The cost is concrete in this benchmark.  The full PLS preprocessing grid
   114	evaluates 600 combinations across five trials, and the Ridge grid evaluates
   115	the same combinations across ten trials, giving 3000 and 6000 candidate fits
   116	per dataset/seed before the final refit.  Repeating this search on small
   117	calibration sets can also make the selected pipeline unstable: the chosen
   118	preprocessing is the winner of a multiplicative loop over folds, operators and
   119	model hyperparameters.  A single operator-adaptive linear fit is easier to
   120	audit because the selected geometry is part of one calibration object with
   121	coefficients recovered on the original wavelength axis.
   122	
   123	This paper focuses on the part of preprocessing that has a simple algebraic
   124	answer.  If a preprocessing step is a fixed linear operator on the wavelength
   125	axis, it can be represented by a matrix $\A$ and moved inside PLS or Ridge
   126	calibration.  The model can then screen a bank of such operators in one fit
   127	rather than refitting every transformed data matrix as an external pipeline.
   128	This does not claim that every preprocessing step is linear.  SNV, MSC, EMSC
   129	and ASLS are fitted or sample-adaptive transformations; they remain
   130	fold-local branches in the experimental protocol.
   131	
   132	The contribution is deliberately narrow.  We derive the PLS covariance
   133	identity and the Ridge operator-kernel identity, instantiate them in AOM-PLS
   134	and AOM-Ridge, and test the resulting models against default PLS/Ridge and
   135	full cartesian preprocessing-HPO baselines.  The question is not whether a
   136	new nonlinear learner can win a benchmark.  The question is whether the
   137	preprocessing search already used with linear chemometric models can be made
   138	faster, simpler and easier to audit.
   139	
   140	The key empirical check is intentionally plain: \texttt{AOM-compact-cv5} and
   141	\texttt{AOMRidge-global-compact-none} use compact strict-linear banks without
   142	the selected ASLS branch, Blender selector or local-neighbourhood machinery.
   143	If these simple AOM baselines already compete with default and preprocessing-
   144	HPO PLS/Ridge, the algebra is doing useful work rather than only moving
   145	complexity into another tuning grid.
   146	
   147	\begin{figure}[t]
   148	  \centering
   149	  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_concept.pdf}
   150	  \caption{External preprocessing screening repeatedly fits transformed
   151	  pipelines.  Operator-adaptive calibration moves strict linear alternatives
   152	  inside the calibration model; fitted or sample-adaptive corrections remain
   153	  fold-local branches.}
   154	  \label{fig:concept}
   155	\end{figure}
   156	
   157	\section{Related work}
   158	
   159	Spectral preprocessing is a central part of chemometrics.  Common tools
   160	include Savitzky--Golay smoothing and derivatives
   161	\citep{savitzky1964smoothing}, Norris--Williams derivatives
   162	\citep{norris1976influence}, SNV \citep{barnes1989standard}, MSC
   163	\citep{geladi1985linearization}, EMSC \citep{martens1991extended} and
   164	penalized baseline smoothers \citep{eilers2003perfect,zhang2010baseline}.
   165	Reviews emphasize that these transformations remove different nuisance
   166	structures and should be chosen with the analytical setting in mind
   167	\citep{rinnan2009review}.
   168	
   169	PLS remains the reference linear method for many NIRS calibration problems
   170	\citep{geladi1986partial,wold2001pls}.  NIPALS and SIMPLS offer two useful
   171	views: one iterative and score-based, the other covariance-based
   172	\citep{dejong1993simpls}.  Ridge regression provides a complementary
   173	regularized linear model \citep{hoerl1970ridge}; in dual form it depends on
   174	the sample Gram matrix, which makes preprocessing a change of kernel geometry
   175	\citep{scholkopf2002learning,hastie2009elements}.
   300	\end{equation}
   301	which gives an original-space coefficient matrix through the matching
   302	operator metric
   303	$\sum_b s_b^2\A_b\transpose\A_b$.
   304	
   305	The plain Ridge baseline is \texttt{AOMRidge-global-compact-none}: one global
   306	selection over the compact strict-linear bank, without Blender mixing or local
   307	neighbourhood routing.  The selected Ridge result uses the Blender selector
   308	(\texttt{AOMRidge-Blender-headline-spxy3}).  It combines several strong
   309	operator geometries through internal validation rather than committing to one
   310	noisy winner.  GCV, local-neighbourhood and additional global compact
   311	selectors are mathematically described in the supplement.
