   413	\section{Results}
   414	
   415	\subsection{PLS regression}
   416	
   417	The plain AOM-PLS regression baseline is \texttt{AOM-compact-cv5}.  Against
   418	PLS-default, it reaches a median RMSEP ratio of 0.996 on $N=52$ datasets with
   419	32/52 wins.  Against the completed PLS-HPO subset, it is near parity, with a
   420	median ratio of 0.990 on $N=32$ and 19/32 wins.  This row has no ASLS branch
   421	and no enlarged operator search; it is the compact strict-linear bank selected
   422	by internal five-fold validation.
   423	
   424	The selected AOM-PLS regression variant is \texttt{ASLS-AOM-compact-cv5}.  It
   425	was selected from the largest AOM-PLS screening denominator and then compared
   426	in the main text with the pre-registered linear references.  Against
   427	PLS-default, it reaches a median RMSEP ratio of 0.985 on $N=52$ datasets with
   428	33/52 wins.  Against the completed PLS-HPO subset, it is essentially tied:
   429	median ratio 1.002 on $N=32$, 15/32 wins.
   430	
   431	This is the intended interpretation.  Full external preprocessing-HPO can
   432	recover the benefit of careful preprocessing, but the gain is obtained by
   433	repeatedly fitting the linear calibration under a much larger search budget.
   434	AOM-PLS makes the strict-linear part of the same decision inside one
   435	coefficient-bearing calibration object.
   436	
   437	\subsection{Ridge regression}
   438	
   439	The plain Ridge AOM baseline is \texttt{AOMRidge-global-compact-none}.  It
   440	improves over default Ridge with a median RMSEP ratio of 0.974 on $N=52$
   441	datasets, 41/52 wins and Holm-adjusted $p=0.002$.  On the completed Ridge-HPO
   442	overlap, it remains close to the grid baseline: median ratio 0.978 on
   443	$N=34$, 21/34 wins.
   444	
   445	The selected Ridge variant is \texttt{AOMRidge-Blender-headline-spxy3}.  It
   446	improves strongly over default Ridge: median RMSEP ratio 0.913 on $N=52$
   447	datasets with 44/52 wins and Holm-adjusted $p=2.9\times10^{-6}$.  It also
   448	improves over the completed Ridge-HPO subset, with median ratio 0.956 on
   449	$N=34$, 27/34 wins and $p=0.011$.
   450	
   451	The Ridge result supports the operator-kernel view.  In Ridge, preprocessing
   452	changes the regularized sample geometry.  Blender is the best main selector
   453	because it combines strong operator geometries through internal validation
   454	while preserving a single deployable Ridge model.
